WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.090
Reading and listening are both
powerful ways to learn a language.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.820
They are also powerful goals
of learning a language.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.639
We want to read, we want
to understand what we hear.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:12.350
We want to understand movies.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.979
And yet the fact of reading in
the language and listening in the

00:00:15.979 --> 00:00:18.069
language helps us learn the language.

00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:22.470
But then the question always comes up,
should we read first or listen first?

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.895
Or people say, I don't
understand anything.

00:00:25.895 --> 00:00:26.695
How can I listen?

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:28.175
I don't know any words.

00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:28.935
How can I read?

00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:30.105
How do I get started?

00:00:30.175 --> 00:00:33.275
So I want to talk today about
the strategies that I use.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.880
For reading and listening and when
it's important to read first, when

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.430
it's important to listen first.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.930
First of all, reading, and it's
a tremendous cognitive feat for

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.510
the brain to be able to read.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.380
So the idea that we can convert
the writing lines on a page

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.409
into meaning is pretty amazing.

00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:57.230
What's also interesting, and this
was pointed out recently in a

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:00.980
short video that I saw by Andrew
Huberman, but it's something that

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.695
I have experienced myself and that
is, when we read, we sub vocalize.

00:01:06.005 --> 00:01:08.235
We actually voice what we're reading.

00:01:08.455 --> 00:01:12.914
If one were to put an amplifier to
measure the firing of those muscles

00:01:12.934 --> 00:01:16.484
in our vocal cords, we'd find that
as we're reading information, we are

00:01:16.494 --> 00:01:18.154
actually speaking that information.

00:01:18.365 --> 00:01:19.695
That's not the same as speaking.

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:24.815
Speaking means creating sentences,
thoughts, utterances, where we

00:01:24.815 --> 00:01:27.955
are drawing on our ability in
the language, our memory of the

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:29.684
language, our model of the language.

00:01:29.804 --> 00:01:30.925
That's speaking.

00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:35.724
Simply reading out loud or sub
vocalizing actually is not speaking.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:40.690
But it's a very good way to move you in
the direction of being able to speak.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.380
So if reading is connected to sub
vocalizing, and we also know that

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.939
essentially the same parts of the brain
are involved in reading or listening, if

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:54.590
we're going to read and we're going to
sub vocalize, we have to know what those

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.990
words or how they are pronounced, to some
extent, even if we pronounce them poorly.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.480
So that would suggest that we
should begin by listening so that

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.150
we get a sense of how the words
are pronounced so that we can then

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.670
effectively sub vocalize while reading.

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:16.185
In simple terms, I would divide
my sort of strategy or approach to

00:02:16.485 --> 00:02:20.345
listening and reading and the order
in which I do them into three stages.

00:02:20.425 --> 00:02:25.374
So the absolute beginner stage, call
it A1 to A2 stage, then the sort of

00:02:25.375 --> 00:02:27.465
intermediate stage, which is B1 really.

00:02:27.465 --> 00:02:29.614
'Cause once you're at B2,
you're kind of launched.

00:02:29.705 --> 00:02:34.065
And then once I'm at B2 or
beyond, what is my strategy?

00:02:34.165 --> 00:02:37.355
At the very beginning, I look at a
sentence of Finnish, as you will see.

00:02:37.515 --> 00:02:38.495
I see words.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.130
If these words are written in the
Latin alphabet or an alphabet that I'm

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.239
familiar with, I can kind of pronounce
it, but as you'll see in Finnish,

00:02:47.279 --> 00:02:50.179
I can't really pronounce it the way
it's pronounced in Finnish, but I

00:02:50.179 --> 00:02:52.420
can kind of make an effort to do so.

00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:57.009
So therefore, as a beginner,
I would, first of all, go at

00:02:57.009 --> 00:02:59.810
this text in sentence view.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.740
So one sentence at a time, and then
I would listen to that sentence...

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:13.455
and then I would try to read that
sentence ... so the order would be listen

00:03:13.455 --> 00:03:18.815
first, then read, but very soon, again,
dealing with one sentence at a time, I

00:03:18.815 --> 00:03:23.845
would try to force myself to read first
and then listen, because we can get lazy.

00:03:23.965 --> 00:03:28.955
If I listen to it, then I'm not trying
hard enough to vocalize those sounds.

00:03:29.105 --> 00:03:34.810
So I would typically say, if I'm doing the
mini stories, maybe after many story two

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:40.649
or three, I would try to make an attempt
at pronouncing the sentence in Finnish

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.070
because I can read the Latin alphabet.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.799
Then I would listen to it and
then I would say it again.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.929
And so this is building up my capability
to sub vocalize so that I can read

00:03:50.084 --> 00:03:51.565
more and more difficult material.

00:03:51.795 --> 00:03:56.355
Remember that all of the skills that
we develop through whatever practices

00:03:56.355 --> 00:04:00.855
we engage in, we're gradually building
up our ability in the language.

00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:02.584
It's very similar to what happens in AI.

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.444
Exposure to a lot of different contexts

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.529
it's going to help us almost predict
what's coming at us and being able to

00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:13.420
predict is also both in the case of
artificial intelligence and in the case

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.479
of learning a language is an important
part of us getting a sense, sort of

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:19.119
a model of what that language is.

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:20.990
So that's at the beginner stage.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:25.219
One sentence at a time, starting
off listening first, then read,

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:26.209
then listen, then read again.

00:04:28.305 --> 00:04:32.075
The next stage, and by the way,
this idea of having a reading and

00:04:32.075 --> 00:04:36.235
listening strategy that depends on
the stage you are in the language, I

00:04:36.235 --> 00:04:37.965
think is a good thing to try to do.

00:04:38.085 --> 00:04:42.284
But the point there is that whether
we're reading or listening, we are

00:04:42.284 --> 00:04:44.305
in fact kind of doing the same thing.

00:04:44.344 --> 00:04:48.445
And the two learning activities
and the two goals of our learning

00:04:48.495 --> 00:04:50.375
very much work hand in hand.

00:04:50.525 --> 00:04:54.925
If we look now at the intermediate stage,
obviously it's easier to listen first.

00:04:54.965 --> 00:04:58.505
I get up in the morning, the easiest thing
for me to do is to listen to something.

00:04:58.785 --> 00:05:01.545
So I'll go grab a podcast,
let's say in Farsi.

00:05:01.665 --> 00:05:04.934
While I'm listening to that, there
is quite a bit there that I didn't

00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:07.495
understand or that I'm kind of
familiar with the words, but I'm

00:05:07.495 --> 00:05:08.704
not entirely sure what they mean.

00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:14.585
So that then triggers my curiosity
to go and read that transcript,

00:05:14.664 --> 00:05:19.655
which I can now do automatically
in LingQ using our Whisper AI.

00:05:20.064 --> 00:05:26.705
So in that situation where I'm B1 and
a half in Persian, I tend to listen

00:05:26.894 --> 00:05:29.034
and then later on, I'll go in and read.

00:05:29.915 --> 00:05:33.115
Also, this issue of intensive
reading versus extensive reading.

00:05:33.385 --> 00:05:35.815
Intensive reading, you're looking
up every word, you're going

00:05:35.815 --> 00:05:37.045
over the sentence a few times.

00:05:37.235 --> 00:05:39.535
Extensive reading, you're just reading.

00:05:39.674 --> 00:05:41.045
And the same applies to listening.

00:05:42.224 --> 00:05:47.444
I have found that at the beginning stage
for me, it's not possible to just, you

00:05:47.625 --> 00:05:52.725
know, go straight into extensive reading,
not looking anything up, just listening.

00:05:52.855 --> 00:05:53.445
I can't do it.

00:05:53.675 --> 00:05:57.555
I have to have a certain level
of vocabulary, a certain level of

00:05:57.614 --> 00:06:02.280
familiarity to be able to move to
more extensive reading and listening,

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.670
no longer in sentence mode perhaps,
certainly not repeating what I'm

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.770
reading or listening, and maybe I don't
even have to read what I listen to.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:12.799
I can read over here and listen over
here, and all the while I'm building

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.300
up my capability in the language.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:19.840
So, the further we are along in the
language, the more we have a tendency

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:24.750
to not repeat, to not go back and
it's better for us not to repeat.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.025
I just wanted to talk a little bit
about, because the question always comes

00:06:28.025 --> 00:06:30.435
up, do you listen first or read first?

00:06:30.564 --> 00:06:33.854
And I think this very much depends on
your level in the language, where you

00:06:33.854 --> 00:06:35.764
are in the language, what you like to do.

00:06:35.875 --> 00:06:39.884
It's in your interest, I think, to develop
a taste for both listening and reading,

00:06:40.154 --> 00:06:45.515
because the greater the variety of paths,
of formats that we use, the better for us.

00:06:45.515 --> 00:06:48.625
Makes things more interesting, and
I think we learn better that way.

